WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:05.100
Let's look at security from the engineer side,
reviewing

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.760
a pull request, understanding

3
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.160
the impact of a finding, and getting a fix

4
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:09.820
ready for review.

5
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.660
We'll start with the security finding in the PR,

6
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.220
follow the change through Change Stack

7
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.560
and security blast radius, then use fix with AI

8
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.560
to prepare a patch.

9
00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:24.560
Here in PR security reviews, I'll open pull
request

10
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.000
4.

11
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.720
This change adds bulk checkout, promotional
pricing, and invoice

12
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:30.160
exports.

13
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.520
We'll focus on the SQL injection finding in
invoice

14
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:33.860
search.

15
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.040
The finding points to a query that inserts
search

16
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.640
text directly into a SQL command.

17
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.980
It also inserts the tenant ID into that command.

18
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.420
A crafted search could change the query and
return

19
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.360
invoices belonging to another customer.

20
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:54.020
I can see the severity, reachability, and
exploitability assessment

21
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.700
along with the code behind the finding.

22
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.020
The recommended fix is to bind both values as

23
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.560
parameters so the database treats them as data.

24
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.680
Let's open the pull request and look at the

25
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:07.820
review comment in context.

26
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.200
From here, I'll choose review in Change Stack.

27
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.820
Change Stack organizes the PR into related
layers.

28
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.720
That lets me review the invoice changes together
and

29
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.300
see how they fit into the rest of the

30
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:20.720
pull request.

31
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.960
Now I'll open security blast radius and select
the

32
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.120
SQL injection concern.

33
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.060
The graph shows the changed components and their
relationships.

34
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.520
For invoice search and export, I can inspect the

35
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:34.400
shared data model.

36
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.620
This code depends on and the web client that

37
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.300
sends searches and receives results.

38
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.900
Those connections help me decide where else to
look.

39
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.120
They don't mean every connected component is
vulnerable.

40
00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.200
I can open the supporting evidence and check
what

41
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.060
each relationship actually involves.

42
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.780
In this case, the important question is whether
customer

43
00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.440
controlled search text can alter the database
query

44
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.960
and cross boundary between tenants.

45
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.380
That gives me a specific behavior to check when

46
00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.140
I review the fix.

47
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.300
Let's address the finding.

48
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.860
I'll open the affected component, choose open in
layer

49
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.000
and return to the review comments beside the
code.

50
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.620
In the comments panel, I'll select fix with AI

51
00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.040
for the SQL injection finding.

52
00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.700
Then I'll review the selected comment and choose
how

53
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.160
the agent should deliver the change.

54
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.620
I'm choosing a separate pull request so I can

55
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.660
inspect the proposed patch before it reaches the
original

56
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.080
branch.

57
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.080
To show the result, let's switch to a complete

58
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:38.180
run of this same fix in our recording
repository.

59
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.240
The patch replaces the interpolated SQL with
bound parameters

60
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.580
for the tenant ID and search pattern.

61
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.300
It also escapes wildcard characters, so a search
for

62
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.040
those characters matches them literally.

63
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.640
The agent added regression tests for injection
attempts and

64
00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:57.160
tenant isolation.

65
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.860
The task reports 71 passing tests with four
unrelated

66
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.200
and expected failures.

67
00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.300
Here's the resulting pull request.

68
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.820
I can inspect the code and the tests before

69
00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.940
deciding whether to merge.

70
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.720
For this demo, we are leaving it open.

71
00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.900
Back on the original pull request, CodeRabbit
has

72
00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:16.640
requested changes.

73
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.380
The application checks passed, but the security
concerns still

74
00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:21.060
needs to be addressed.

75
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.540
This demo branch doesn't have protection
enabled, so the

76
00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.800
request for changes isn't an enforcement block.

77
00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.320
With branch rules, a team can require the checks

78
00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.980
and approvals it needs before accepting a
change.

79
00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.900
We followed a security finding from the PR to

80
00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:38.720
its wider impact,

81
00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.260
prepared a proposed fix and identified what
needs review

82
00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:43.520
before merging.
